{"id":1213,"date":"2020-11-24T10:58:30","date_gmt":"2020-11-24T18:58:30","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1213"},"modified":"2024-09-01T20:26:57","modified_gmt":"2024-09-02T03:26:57","slug":"codrone-arduino-while-loops","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-while-loops\/","title":{"rendered":"While Loops"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73af61\" name=\"6a2215d73af61\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are loops?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Loops let you repeat sections of your code. They&#8217;re a great shortcut &#8212; without them, you might have to write the same lines over and over again. You&#8217;ve already been using loops in your CoDrone programs &#8212; everything you use in void loop repeats!<\/p>\n<p>In this lesson you&#8217;ll be learning about while loops, and in the next lesson, you&#8217;ll be learning about for loops. All of them are conditional loops, so they repeat only for as long the condition that you set is true.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73b089\" name=\"6a2215d73b089\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]While loops repeat a set of code while a certain condition you set is true. You use these in your life all of the time! For example: while it is raining, I will use an umbrella.<\/p>\n<p>This is what while loops usually look like:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">while(condition) {\r\n    statement;\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is what all of that means:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The condition is what needs to be true for the while loop to start.<\/li>\n<li>The statement is what will happen if the condition is true, and it will be checked every time the loop goes through.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>If the condition is false, the program will exit the while loop.<\/p>\n<p>So this is what the real-life example from above would look like in a while loop:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">while(it is raining) {\r\n    I will use an umbrella;\r\n}<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>If you&#8217;re experiencing some deja vu with this, you might be remembering the lesson on if statements. The conditionals that they use are exactly the same as the conditionals you&#8217;ll be using for while and for loops!<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73b11e\" name=\"6a2215d73b11e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>While loop example<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Upload this code to your remote and open your serial monitor to see what happens!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int x;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {\r\n    Serial.begin(9600);\r\n    delay(3000);\r\n} \r\nvoid loop() {\r\n    while (x &lt; 3) {\r\n        x = x + 1;\r\n        Serial.println(x);\r\n    }\r\n    x = 0;\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Create an int variable named x.<\/li>\n<li>Establish communication at a baud rate of 9600.<\/li>\n<li>Wait 3 seconds.<\/li>\n<li>While x is less than 3, add 1 to x.<\/li>\n<li>Print x&#8217;s value in the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<li>When the loop is exited, set x to 0.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Your serial monitor should look like the image below!<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1214&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Bonus: try moving <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">x = x + 1<\/code> outside of the while loop so your program looks like this:<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">int x;\r\n\r\nvoid setup() {    \r\n    Serial.begin(9600);    \r\n    delay(3000);\r\n}    \r\n\r\nvoid loop() {        \r\n    x = x + 1;    \r\n        while (x &lt; 3) {        \r\n            Serial.println(x);    \r\n        }    \r\n    x = 0;\r\n}<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>If you open up your serial monitor, you&#8217;ll just get a bunch of 1s! The reason for that is because the statement that changes the value of x is outside of the while loop, so x never updates.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73b193\" name=\"6a2215d73b193\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using sensors as conditions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You can use your remote control&#8217;s sensors in your loops! However, because loops only read within the loop and the condition, this is where you should include the sensor update code.<\/p>\n<p>There are two ways to do this:<\/p>\n<p>1. You can store the variable inside the condition and explicitly tell the variable to update in the loop.<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">while (bt1){\r\n    \/\/Statement\r\n    bt1 = digitalRead(11);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>2. You can completely skip the variable and do a sensor check inside the condition.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">while (digitalRead(11)){\r\n    \/\/Statement\r\n}<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73b200\" name=\"6a2215d73b200\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Initiators<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]There might be times when you don&#8217;t want your program to run until you send some type of start signal. One way to do this is with a flag, which you already learned about, but you can also use a while loop to pause your code and wait for input.<\/p>\n<p>Upload this code to your remote control, open your serial monitor, and see what happens!<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">void setup() {\r\n    Serial.begin(9600);\r\n    pinMode(11, INPUT); \r\n    while (!digitalRead(11)) {\r\n        Serial.println(\"Waiting...\");\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\r\nvoid loop() {\r\n    Serial.println(\"Started\");\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>The pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Establish communication at a baud rate of 9600.<\/li>\n<li>Set up pin 11 as an input.<\/li>\n<li>While sensor 11 is not covered, print &#8220;Waiting&#8230;&#8221; in the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<li>When sensor 11 is covered, print &#8220;Started&#8221; in the serial monitor.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1215&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73b323\" name=\"6a2215d73b323\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Hover code<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]If you want to be able to pause your program so your CoDrone can hover, try this code! It uses sensor 17 in a while loop so that when it&#8217;s covered, the CoDrone will pause and hover in place. Pausing is great when you don&#8217;t want to crash into a moving object but also don&#8217;t want to completely stop your code.<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\nint StartFlag;\r\nunsigned long StartTime; \r\n\r\nvoid setup() { \r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200); \r\n    CoDrone.pair(); \r\n    \r\n    CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n} \r\n\r\nvoid loop() { \r\n    byte bt1 = digitalRead(11); \r\n    byte bt2 = digitalRead(12); \r\n    byte bt7 = digitalRead(17); \r\n    byte bt8 = digitalRead(18);  \r\n    if (bt8) { \r\n        StartFlag = 1; \r\n        StartTime = millis(); \r\n    }  \r\n    if (StartFlag == 1) { \r\n        if (millis() - StartTime &lt; 2000) { \r\n            CoDrone.setThrottle(80);\r\n            CoDrone.move(); \r\n        } \r\n        else if ( millis() - StartTime &lt; 5000) { \r\n            CoDrone.setPitch(70); \r\n            CoDrone.setThrottle(0);\r\n            CoDrone.move(); \r\n        } \r\n        else { \r\n            CoDrone.land(); \r\n            StartFlag = 0; \r\n        } \r\n    }  \r\n    if ( bt1) { \r\n        StartFlag = 0; \r\n        CoDrone.emergencyStop(); \r\n    }  \r\n    while (bt7) { \r\n        CoDrone.move(); \r\n        bt7 = digitalRead(17); \r\n    } \r\n}<\/pre>\n<div class=\"activity2__rtf\">\n<p>This is the pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone library.<\/li>\n<li>Create an int variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartFlag<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>Create an unsigned long variable called <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartTime<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>Establish communication at a baud rate of 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Connect to a nearby drone.<\/li>\n<li>Take off for two seconds<\/li>\n<li>Set up buttons 11, 12, 17, and 18 as inputs for sensors 11, 12, 17, and 18.<\/li>\n<li>If sensor 18 is covered, set <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartFlag<\/code> as 1 and store the starting milliseconds in <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartTime<\/code>.<\/li>\n<li>If <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartFlag<\/code> is 1 and if the current milliseconds minus <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartTime<\/code>&#8216;s value is less than 2000, throttle up at 80.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartFlag<\/code> is 1 and if the current milliseconds minus <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartTime<\/code>&#8216;s value is less than 5000, pitch forward at 70.<\/li>\n<li>Else, if <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartFlag<\/code> is 1, land and set <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartFlag<\/code> to 0.<\/li>\n<li>If sensor 11 is covered, set <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">StartFlag<\/code> to 0 and stop the CoDrone.<\/li>\n<li>While sensor 17 is covered, pause the CoDrone.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>You might be thinking, &#8220;But Robolink! Why can&#8217;t I just use an if statement?!&#8221; The rest of the code would still read at the same time, and because of all of the if and else if statements you&#8217;re using, your CoDrone would receive a lot of conflicting commands.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"activity2__spacer\">\n<div class=\"activity2__question\">\n<div class=\"styled-checkbox activity2__button-checkbox\"><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73b3c7\" name=\"6a2215d73b3c7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Quick color mode<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]While loops can also act like a switch since they&#8217;re kind of like a program within your program. This code works like a normal remote control code, but while sensor 18 is covered, it will also change your CoDrone&#8217;s LEDs!<\/p>\n<div class=\"js-snippet-hold\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"cpp\">#include &lt;CoDrone.h&gt;\r\n\r\nvoid setup()\r\n{\r\n    CoDrone.begin(115200);\r\n    CoDrone.pair(); \r\n    \r\n    CoDrone.takeoff();\r\n} \r\nvoid loop()\r\n{\r\n    byte bt1 = digitalRead(11);\r\n    byte bt4 = digitalRead(14);\r\n    byte bt8 = digitalRead(18);\r\n    LEDs(0,0);\r\n    if (bt1) {\r\n        CoDrone.emergencyStop();\r\n    } \r\n    if (PAIRING == true) {\r\n        YAW = -1 * CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(22));\r\n        THROTTLE = CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(23));\r\n        ROLL = -1 * CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(24));\r\n        PITCH = CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(25));\r\n        CoDrone.move();\r\n    } \r\n    while (bt8) {\r\n        bt8 = digitalRead(18);\r\n        LEDs(1,1);\r\n        if (CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(22)) &gt; 50) {\r\n            CoDrone.setArmLED(255, 255, 0, DIMMING); \/\/yellow\r\n        }\r\n        else if (CoDrone.AnalogScaleChange(analogRead(22)) &lt; -50 ) {\r\n            CoDrone.setArmLED(0, 0, 255, DIMMING); \/\/blue\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n} \r\nvoid LEDs(int p13, int p16) {\r\n    digitalWrite(13, p13);\r\n    digitalWrite(16, p16);\r\n}<\/pre>\n<p>The pseudocode:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Include the CoDrone library.<\/li>\n<li>Establish communication at a baud rate of 115200.<\/li>\n<li>Connect to a nearby drone.<\/li>\n<li>Take off for two seconds.<\/li>\n<li>Set buttons 11, 14, and 18 as sensors 11, 14, and 18.<\/li>\n<li>Turn the LEDs off.<\/li>\n<li>If sensor 11 is covered, stop the CoDrone.<\/li>\n<li>If the CoDrone pairs, get the correct values for your joysticks for yaw, throttle, roll, and pitch.<\/li>\n<li>While sensor 18 is covered, turn the LEDs on.<\/li>\n<li>If sensor 18 is covered and if the value for the yaw is greater than 50, set the CoDrone&#8217;s arm LEDs to dim yellow.<\/li>\n<li>If sensor 18 is covered and if the value for the yaw is less than -50, set the CoDrone&#8217;s arm LEDs to dim blue.<\/li>\n<li>Create variables for pins 13 and 16 and turn LEDs 13 and 16 on.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215d73b439\" name=\"6a2215d73b439\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<strong>Challenge: \u00a0While We\u2019re At It&#8230;<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>Create an\u00a0quick switch mode that uses your joysticks to set certain options\u00a0while covering sensor 18.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Rules:<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>When sensor 18 is not covered:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal remote control program.<\/li>\n<li>Sensor 11 should be a kill switch.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>When sensor 18 is covered:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The left joystick controls 4 different LED color modes. For example: Up makes arms yellow, Right makes arms blue, Down makes eyes green, Left makes eyes white.<\/li>\n<li>Limit the values for throttle, pitch, yaw, and roll from 0 to 100.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>Bonus: include buzzers![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 What are loops? [vc_column_text]Loops let you repeat sections of your code. They're a great shortcut --- without them, you might have to write the same lines over&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/codrone-arduino-while-loops\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1749,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"easy","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":804,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1815,1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,1392,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2195,2163,2164,2165,2123,2118,2122,2203],"class_list":["post-1213","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-arduino","lesson-tag-codrone","lesson-tag-codrone-pro","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-loops","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-while-loops","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1213","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":1,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1213\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":1216,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1213\/revisions\/1216"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1749"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1213"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=1213"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=1213"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}